the efficiency of the process decays exponentially. The ability to address this by isolating remaining methylcyclohexane from toluene without the need for an energy-consuming phase change is therefore critical. Moreover, the separation of benzene and cyclohexane following hydrogenation reactions is an extremely difficult, expensive and industrially important task in the chemical industry.5,6
The similar molecular sizes and the small difference in the boiling points of cyclic hydrocarbons makes conventional distillation techniques practically impossible. This is illustrated by the boiling points of benzene and cyclohexane (80 °C and 81 °C, respectively) and of toluene and methylcyclohexane (100 °C and 101 °C, respectively).1 An alternative separation strategy is to exploit the differences of cyclic aliphatic and aromatic molecules when upon physisorption to porous adsorbents.1 Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are emerging porous materials that have recently been investigated as solid adsorbents for the separations of benzene and cyclohexane.7-12 These MOFs encourage selectivity through exploitation of the spatial configurational orientations of the two molecules, i.e. the planar π-cloud of benzene and the non-planar configurations of cyclohexane.7 MOFs investigated thus far have shown preference for benzene adsorption over cyclohexane adsorption due to the extensive π-π or Lewis acid-base interactions between the adsorbate and the framework.1, 7-13
Recently, we have explored the use of cubane-1,4-dicarboxylate (cdc) as an aliphatic linker for creating MOFs with contoured pore environments.14 The H2cdc ligand is readily synthesized using flow chemistry procedures and shares the length and ditopic connectivity of benzene-1,4-dicarboxylic acid (H2bdc) (Scheme S1).15 Cubane-1,4-dicarboxylic acid differs from other aliphatic linkers in terms of its inherit rigidity, meaning that aliphatic MOFs, which are stable upon desolvation, can be developed and investigated.14 Drawing on the isoreticular principle of MOF synthesis,16 CUB-5, [Zn2O(cdc)3], was produced, an aliphatic analogue of MOF-5, starting from H2cdc.14 Interestingly, the aliphatic pore environment of CUB-5 distinguished its guest uptake behaviour from MOF-5.
CUB-5 presents a very high preference for benzene adsorption at low partial pressures over other \( C_{6} \) hydrocarbons; an intriguing separation characteristic relevant to the separation of benzene from gasoline. This indicates that other factors besides π—π interactions govern adsorption behaviors within the framework. Given these attractive properties, we considered incorporating cdc into ternary and quaternary multicomponent MOFs, which would enable the exploitation of larger and more diverse microporous environments for hydrocarbon separations.
The construction of multicomponent MOFs offers an opportunity to generate complex structures and pore environments for sophisticated applications that are difficult to produce in a single component system. Multicomponent MOFs
position several distinct, yet complementary, linkers in specific lattice sites and are free from randomness and disorder (Figure 1). This leads to ordered pore environments and the emergence of structure-property relationships. Multivariate MOFs contrast from multicomponent MOFs, and are constructed from linkers with the same backbone, albeit differing pendant groups. These linkers tend to become scrambled in the lattice due to lack of distinction, which leads to an absence of structural regularity and multiple types of pore.
Multicomponent MOFs have emerging applications in catalysis, luminescence and gas storage. In order to form multicomponent MOFs judicious selection of the linkers is essential (Figure 1, Scheme S1).
