In the case of zinc(II)-carboxylate frameworks, the symmetry and metrics of the linkers govern their mutual compatibility. For example, in UMCM-1 (University of Michigan Crystalline Material-1), the ternary prototype of this class, benzene-1,3,5- tribenzoic acid \((\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{b t b})\) co-assembles with \(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{b d c}\) into a regular lattice with a muo topology that is free from disorder and defects (Scheme S2).35,36
Of late, quaternary members of the family have emerged based on a combination of btb, bdc and biphenyl-4,4'- dicarboxylate (bpdc) linkers. MUF-7 (MUF = Massey University Framework) was the first member of this series. MUF-7 exhibits an ith-d topology, with pores that can be tuned in shape and chemical functionality by introducing functional groups to the linkers.37 Replacing the btb linker with a more rigid, truxene centered linker that retains the same metrics, such as \(5,5^{\prime},10,10^{\prime},15,15^{\prime}\)-hexamethyltruxene-2,7,12-tricarboxylic acid (H3hmtt), produces MOFs that are particularly stable and easily prepared and derivatised.38 Referred to as MUF-7', these frameworks are notable for their resilience to water vapor and aqueous environments. To date, multicomponent zinc(II)-carboxylate MOFs have been confined to aromatic linkers, which inherently limits the chemical make-up of the pore environment.
Here, we present a group of multicomponent MOFs comprising the cubane linker, generated to investigate two key areas:
- Can the functional properties of multicomponent MOFs also encompass hydrocarbon separations?- Can we verify that enhanced host-guest interactions with the cubane linker exist even in a framework with multiple pore environments?
We herein report the realization of these goals in the form of a range of multicomponent zinc(II)-carboxylate frameworks that contain the cubane linker.
## Results and Discussion
Our investigations into multicomponent MOFs built up from \(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{c d c}\) began with the synthesis of muo type frameworks analogous to UMCM-1 \(([\mathsf{Z n}_{4}\mathsf{O}(\mathsf{b d c})(\mathsf{b t b})_{4/3}])\) . UMCM-1 contains both micropores and mesopores and is prepared by a solvothermal reaction between \(\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{b t b}\) \(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{b d c}\) and \(\mathsf{Z n}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}\)
in DEF. The mole ratio of \(\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{b t b}\) and \(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{b d c}\) is crucial to suppress undesired phases, such as MOF-5 \(([Z\mathsf{n}_{4}0(\mathsf{b d c})_{3}])\) and MOF-177 \(([Z\mathsf{n}_{4}0(\mathsf{b t b})_{2})]\) , and exclusively obtain phase-pure UMCM-1. To synthesize a UMCM-1 type framework incorporating the cubane linker, \(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{c d c}\) replaced \(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{b d c}\) in the reaction mixture while maintaining the ligand molar ratio previously optimized for phase-pure UMCM-1. Large, prismatic crystals were isolated. Single crystal X-ray diffraction (SCXRD) revealed these to be the previously-reported CUB-5, \([Z\mathsf{n}_{4}0(\mathsf{c d c})_{3}])\) .14 This implies that the cdc linker outcompetes btb, therefore higher molar concentrations of \(\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{b t b}\) were required. By systematically screening the molar ratio of these linkers a phase-pure material with a needle-like morphology was isolated.
Single crystal X-ray diffraction (SCXRD) of these crystals revealed them to be analogous to UMCM-1 with the formula \([Z\mathsf{n}_{4}0(\mathsf{c d c})(\mathsf{b t b})_{4/3}]\) . This framework is referred to as CUB-20. CUB-20 crystallizes in the hexagonal space group \(\mathsf{P6}_{3}\mathsf{m}\) (Figure 1), with each \(\mathsf{Z n}_{4}\mathsf{O}\) cluster connected by two cdc linkers and four btb linkers (Table 1, Table S1). These clusters are six-connected and adopt an octahedral geometry with the two ditopic linkers lying adjacent to one another, analogous to the coordination environment observed within UMCM-1. The overall muo topology provides a framework with large mesopores surrounded radially by micropores, with diameters of \(33.6\,\mathring{\mathsf{A}}\) and \(26.1\,\mathring{\mathsf{A}}\) , respectively. These pore dimensions are smaller than UMCM-1, further reflecting the slightly shorter length of the cdc ditopic linker compared to bdc (Table 1). Bulk purity was confirmed using powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) (Figure S3) however CUB-20 exhibited the same sensitivity to moisture as observed for UMCM-1, with framework degradation under ambient conditions.35,36,39
Because we are interested in the practical applications of multicomponent cubane MOFs for hydrocarbon separations, we turned to alternative frameworks that are known to exhibit better stability. A linker with a truxene core, \(\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{b t b}\) was employed as a substitute for \(\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{b t b}\) in an attempt to enhance the moisture stability of CUB-20. \(\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{b t b}\) and \(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{c d c}\) and \(\mathsf{Z n}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}\) were combined in DEF at \(85\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) , resulting in the formation of crystals of plate morphology. Single crystal X-ray diffraction proved difficult, with only some crystals being of sufficient quality. The plate like crystals were identified as \([Z\mathsf{n}_{4}0(\mathsf{c d c})(\mathsf{h m t t})_{4/3}]\) (CUB-21), analogous to CUB-20. CUB-21 crystallizes in the same
space group as UMCM-1 \((P\mathfrak{G}_{3}m)\) with the same muo framework topology. CUB-21 also exhibits the smallest pore diameters, with mesopores and micropores being 32.5 Å and 25.4 Å, respectively (Figure 1). However, sample digestion of the plate only phases and subsequent analysis using \(^1\mathsf{H}\) NMR spectroscopy revealed a very high amount of \(\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{h m t t}\) relative to cdc, indicating an additional phase present in the bulk crystalline product. Despite multiple synthetic attempts, isolation of a pure phase of CUB-21 could not be achieved.
The aim of our investigation into cubane multicomponent MOF systems was to ascertain whether or not there would be significant differences in the gas and vapour adsorption behavior between the parent MOF and the cubane MOF derivative due to the influence of the bulky, aliphatic linker. Therefore, the inherit moisture sensitivity and difficulty in isolating phase pure CUB-20 and CUB-21 material precluded them from further gas and vapor sorption studies due to the cumbersome procedures required to scale-up this material in an industrial setting. Quaternary multicomponent systems comprising rigid, tritopic linkers, however, have shown enhanced stability over ternary systems and also offer greater opportunity to modify the pore environment due to the use of an additional linker.38 Investigations into forming a cubane analogue of the quaternary MOF system MUF-77, \([Z\mathsf{n}_{4}\mathsf{O}(\mathsf{h m t t})_{4/3}(\mathsf{b p d c})_{1/2}(\mathsf{b c d c})_{1/2}]\), were initiated due to its excellent stability to moisture and the ease of preparing phase-pure analogs. The successful formation of this material is found through the convergence of three topologically and geometrically distinct carboxylate linkers that are mutually compatible for inclusion into a crystalline lattice.
The slightly different lengths of the cdc and bdc linkers raised questions as to the compatibility of the cdc, bpdca and hmtt linkers (Scheme S1). For cdc to be successfully adopted within the MUF-77 architecture, it would occupy the bdc sites and not perturb the inclusion of bpdca and hmtt. To explore this approach experimentally, a solvothermal reaction of \(Z\mathsf{n}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}\cdot6\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}\) with \(\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{h m t t}\), \(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{b p d c}\) and \(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{c d c}\) in DEF at \(85~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) afforded large, colourless prism crystals. These crystals, herein denoted as CUB-30 \(([Z\mathsf{n}_{4}\mathsf{O}(\mathsf{h m t t})_{4/3}(\mathsf{b p d c})_{1/2}(\mathsf{c d c})_{1/2}])\), were structurally analogous to MUF-77 where the bdc has been replaced by cdc. CUB-30 crystallizes in the same \(P m{-}3\) space group as MUF-77, and exhibits three distinct cavities (Table 1). There are two large dodecahedral cavities, where one cavity is lined by hmtt and cdc, and the other cavity with hmtt and bpdca, and a smaller tetrahedral type cavity, lined with all three linkers (Figure 1). Notably, the difference in length of cdc and bdc is reflected in the shorter distance between the \(Z\mathsf{n}_{4}\mathsf{O}\) clusters within the frameworks, with \(Z\mathsf{n}_{4}\mathsf{O}-Z\mathsf{n}_{4}\mathsf{O}\) distances of \(12.84~\mathring{\mathsf{A}}\) and \(12.78~\mathring{\mathsf{A}}\) in MUF-77 and CUB-30, respectively (Table 1). Bulk phase purity was confirmed through \(^1\mathsf{H}\) NMR analysis of the digested, evacuated framework, which demonstrated that the linkers are present in precisely the same ratio as that given by the framework stoichiometry (hmtt:bpdccdc = 4 : 1.5 : 1.5) and an overall formula of \([Z\mathsf{n}_{4}\mathsf{O}(\mathsf{h m t t})_{4/3}(\mathsf{b p d c})_{1/2}(\mathsf{c d c})_{1/2}]\) was determined (Figure S1). PXRD was also consistent with the presence of just one phase and additional PXRD studies highlighted the remarkable stability of CUB-30 to moisture (Figure S4).
To determine if there is any preference for either of the short ditopic linkers to be adopted within MUF-77, an additional
solvothermal reaction was performed using both \(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{c d c}\) and \(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{b d c}\) in a 1:1 molar ratio during synthesis. This reaction afforded large, colorless crystals with the same morphology as CUB-30, and subsequent structural analysis identified these crystals as analogous to MUF-77, crystallizing in the space group \(P m{-}3\).
Interestingly the cell dimensions of these crystals sat perfectly between those observed for MUF-77 and CUB-30 (Table 1) and the material shows bulk phase purity using PXRD. To ensure a new MOF had formed and not a 1:1 mixture of MUF-77 and CUB-30, the single crystals used for structural analysis were each subjected to digestion and subsequent \(^1\mathsf{H}\) NMR analysis. Integration of its \(^1\mathsf{H}\) NMR spectrum revealed a 1:1 ratio of bdc and cdc present within the crystal, providing a MOF formula of \([Z\mathsf{n}_{4}\mathsf{O}(\mathsf{h m t t})_{4/3}(\mathsf{b p d c})_{1/2}(\mathsf{b d c})_{1/4}(\mathsf{c d c})_{1/4}]\) (Figure S2). This multivariate multicomponent framework is denoted as CUB-31 (Figure 1). CUB-31 crystallizes in the space group \(P m{-}3\), analogous to CUB-30, with a 1:1 ratio of bdc:1,4-cdc in the framework. The distance observed between \(Z\mathsf{n}_{4}\mathsf{O}\) clusters within the framework, \(12.82(\pm0.04)\) Å, is situated between the
distances observed within CUB-30 and MUF-77, as anticipated. PXRD measurements recorded for both CUB-30 and CUB-31 over a period of six weeks highlight their remarkable stability relative humidity (Figures S4 and S5). Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) further exemplifies the robustness of the frameworks with minimal to no weight loss up to temperatures of \(400\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (Figure S6). This stability, uncommon to many other \(\mathsf{Z n}_{4}\mathsf{O}\)-carboxylate frameworks, provides an opportunity for facile analysis of the gas and vapor sorption capabilities of this system.
Further investigation into the CUB-30 framework will thereby provide a direct comparison to the parent framework, MUF-77, and indicate the effect of substituting a third of the framework with an aliphatic linker. The surface areas, total pore volumes, and propensity of CUB-30 and CUB-31 to take up various molecular guests were investigated using gas adsorption techniques (Table 1). Guest molecules that may be occluded within the frameworks were removed through solvent exchange using DCM, where the materials were subsequently activated at \(85\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for 24 hours. \(\mathsf{N}_{2}\) adsorption isotherms recorded at \(77\ K\) reveal a Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface area of 2930 \(\mathsf{m}^{2}\ \mathsf{g}^{-1}\) for CUB-30 which is lower than MUF-77 due to the bulkiness of the cubane ligand in the framework (Figure 2a). Interestingly, the BET surface area of CUB-31 was determined as \(3260\ \mathsf{m}^{2}\ \mathsf{g}^{-1}\), lying between MUF-77 and CUB-30 (Figure 2a, Section S7). Experimental pore volumes of CUB-30 and CUB-31 were \(1.54\ \mathsf{c m}^{3}\ \mathsf{g}^{-1}\) and \(1.68\ \mathsf{c m}^{3}\ \mathsf{g}^{-1}\), calculated from \(\mathsf{N}_{2}\) adsorption isotherms at \(77\ K\) at \(P/P_{0}=0.995\).
These are lower than the experimental pore volume of MUF-77, \(1.85\ \mathsf{c m}^{3}\ \mathsf{g}^{-1}\), due to the presence of the bulky cdc linker, and notably the CUB-31 pore volume falls between MUF-77 and CUB-30. Pore size distribution (PSD) of MUF-77 and CUB-30 was theoretically calculated to identify the smaller pores within the frameworks (Figure 2b). Three pore sizes, one smaller and two larger, are evident and reflect the distribution seen in the structural models produced by SCXRD. Interestingly, the small tetrahedral pore of CUB-30 has a smaller pore width than the MUF-77 framework, as reflected in the shorter \(\mathsf{Z n}_{4}\mathsf{O}-\mathsf{Z n}_{4}\mathsf{O}\) distance between the short ditopic linkers.
Additional gas adsorption measurements were recorded on CUB-30 using \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) and \(\mathsf{C H}_{4}\) to further investigate the differences between CUB-30 and MUF-77. CUB-30 adsorbs 39.6 and 23.6 \(\mathsf{c m}^{3}\,\mathsf{g}^{-1}\) of \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) at \(273\ \mathsf{K}\) and \(293\ \mathsf{K}\), respectively (Figures S7 and S8). The volume of \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) gas is higher compared with the analogous MUF-77 (adsorption capacities of ca. 37 and \(19\ \mathsf{c m}^{3}\ \mathsf{g}^{-1}\) of \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) at \(273\ \mathsf{K}\) and \(293\ \mathsf{K}\), respectively). The higher adsorption in CUB-30 at these pressures is attributed to the greater number of adsorption sites within the MOF. Contrariwise, the adsorption of \(\mathsf{C H}_{4}\) within CUB-30 is quite similar to MUF-77, with an uptake of \(11.7\ \mathsf{c m}^{3}\ \mathsf{g}^{-1}\) in CUB-30 and 13 and \(9\ \mathsf{c m}^{3}\ \mathsf{g}^{-1}\) in MUF-77 at \(273\ \mathsf{K}\) and \(293\ \mathsf{K}\), respectively (Figure S9). The enthalpy of adsorption of both gases within the frameworks is lower in CUB-30 than in MUF-77, most likely due to the protruding methine units associated with the CUB-30 framework (Section S7).
Single component vapor sorption experiments were performed to explore the effect the cubane linker has on the pore environment of the quaternary frameworks. A range of hydrocarbons were selected which included cyclic hydrocarbons (benzene, cyclohexane, toluene and methylcyclohexane), linear hydrocarbons (n-hexane and n-heptane) and branched hydrocarbons (i-hexane and 2,3-dimethylpentane).
All adsorption isotherms show a sharp increase in adsorption at low partial pressures and exhibit an S-shaped profile due to the cooperative effects between guest molecules (Section S8, Figures S12 – S17). There are two exciting separation applications that emerge from this research. Of significant interest is the high preference of cyclohexane adsorption over benzene by CUB-30 at low partial pressures. The separation of cyclohexane and benzene is a particularly important, energy intensive and expensive process due to their similar boiling points and kinetic diameters, \(5.85\,\mathrm{\AA}\) and \(6.0\,\mathrm{\AA}\) for benzene and cyclohexane respectively (Figure S24). The comparable boiling points excludes fractional distillation as an efficient separation process for these two chemicals, however adsorptive separation is an attractive alternative. Some MOFs have been investigated with this particular separation application goal, all providing MOFs selective for benzene adsorption in favour of cyclohexane.7, 9-11, 40, 41 To the best of our knowledge, CUB-30 is the first MOF material to show selective adsorption of cyclohexane over benzene, and as such broadens the scope for MOFs to be used in the removal of low concentrations of cyclohexane from mixed benzene/cyclohexane systems. Both cyclohexane and benzene show a preference of adsorption within CUB-30 at low pressures compared with MUF-77, with a
steady increase of adsorption beginning at \(0.27\,\mathrm{\kPa}\) and 0.44 kPa respectively within CUB-30 (Figures 3a and 3b). The adsorption of both hydrocarbons within MUF-77 is more gradual and less rapid. Interestingly, there is a reversal of adsorption preference from CUB-30 to MUF-77 at 1.0 and \(1.2\,\mathrm{\kPa}\) for cyclohexane and benzene respectively. This is due to larger void space in MUF-77. To accurately compare the adsorption capacities of two MOFs, the adsorption amount was normalized by the surface area of each material at \(0.5\,\mathrm{kPa}\), 1 kPa and \(6\,\mathrm{kPa}\) (Figures 3c, S18 and S19). The normalized capacities at \(0.5\,\mathrm{kPa}\) immediately illustrate the high affinity of CUB-30 for benzene and cyclohexane. Overall, the greatest difference in cyclohexane adsorption capacities occurs at \(0.5\,\mathrm{kPa}\), where CUB-30 outperforms MUF-77 by adsorbing 5 times more cyclohexane. In the case of benzene adsorption, at \(0.7\,\mathrm{\kPa}\) (the greatest difference in adsorption profiles), CUB-30 adsorbs 2.5 times more benzene.
The preference for cyclohexane adsorption in CUB-30 with respect to MUF-77 at low partial pressures can be assigned to the different pore environment of the smaller dodecahedral pore. DFT-D3 calculations were employed to further understand the interaction of benzene and cyclohexane with CUB-30. The DFT-D3 binding energies of benzene and cyclohexane for CUB-30 are \(-43.0\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol}^{-1}\) and \(-55.7\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol}^{-1}\), respectively, revealing a substantial difference in the binding energy of approximately \(12\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol}^{-1}\) for the most favourable adsorption sites. These differences in binding energies can be explained through close examination of the interaction each guest has with the framework. There are substantially more van der Waals interactions between the guest and the CUB-30 framework compared with the MUF-77 framework due to the presence of the bulky cubane linker providing additional sites for interaction. Close van der Waals interactions of below \(3.4\,\mathrm{\AA}\) are highlighted in Figure 4 and Figures S21 and S22. The most favourable adsorption site for cyclohexane lies within the dodecahedral cavity lined with bdc or ddc linkers, whereas that for the benzene molecule lies within the same dodecahedral cavity of CUB-30 and the smaller tetrahedral cavity in MUF-77. This difference in adsorption preference and location can be attributed to the
increased molecular flexibility cyclohexane has compared with benzene. This flexibility allows cyclohexane to contort itself when in close proximity with the walls of the MOFs to maximise its intermolecular interactions.
The selectivity of adsorption of equimolar cyclohexane/benzene (Cy/Bz) mixtures in MUF-77 and CUB-30 was calculated using Ideal Adsorbed Solution Theory (IAST). CUB-30 displays superb selectivity for cyclohexane adsorption at low pressures, where MUF-77 exhibits negligible selectivity (Figure 5). Selective adsorption of Cy/Bz still outcompetes the capabilities of MUF-77 even at pressures of \(300\;\mathsf{kPa}\) (Figure S23). This indicates that the selectivity will be maintained in liquid separations. The preferential adsorption of cyclohexane over benzene in CUB-30 is a remarkable result and the first example of selective cyclohexane adsorption over benzene adsorption within a MOF.
Motivated by the high selectivity of CUB-30 for cyclohexane over benzene, breakthrough curves were simulated to further investigate the ability of CUB-30 to separate cyclohexane and benzene under dynamic conditions. For an equimolar mixture of Cy/Bz at total pressure of \(100\;\mathsf{kPa}\) and partial pressure of \(0.5\;\mathsf{kPa}\) for both hydrocarbons (Figure 6) benzene is eluted through the CUB-30 bed first. This yields an outflow of pure benzene. Conversely, because cyclohexane is more efficiently adsorbed by the CUB-30, cyclohexane breaks through following a substantial time lapse of 3.5 hours. These results indicate that CUB-30 can efficiently trap cyclohexane under dynamic conditions. These breakthrough times were also simulated for MUF-77 and compared with that of CUB-30. As it can be seen from Figure 6, the difference in breakthrough time for MUF-77 is nearly a quarter of that of CUB-30.
Toluene and methylcyclohexane show similar adsorption profiles, with a preference for adsorption within CUB-30 at low pressures, and a steep onset of adsorption at 0.08 and \(0.16\;\mathsf{kPa}\), respectively (Figure 3b). The ability of CUB-30 to adsorb toluene and methylcyclohexane at low pressures is the other significant result. Often the separation of unreacted toluene from a liquid hydrogen feedstock is too expensive to perform and therefore the feedstock has a limited lifetime due to eventual pollution with undesired materials. The dehydrogenation stage of methylcyclohexane conversion to benzene often does not reach completion, and therefore the separation of unreacted toluene from methylcyclohexane, before transport, would contribute substantial savings for this hydrogen transport system.
