As observed for the benzene and cyclohexane profiles, there is a switch in adsorption preference for each vapour as the pressure increases, due greater pore volume of the MUF-77 framework. The greatest difference in adsorption capacity between the two frameworks for toluene occurs at \(0.14\;\mathsf{kPa}\), where CUB-30 adsorbs 2.8 equivalents more toluene. At \(0.25\;\mathsf{kPa}\), 3 equivalents of methylcyclohexane is adsorbed within CUB-30 compared with MUF-77. Remarkably, both adsorption profiles of the cyclic hydrocarbons show a low pressure separation of benzene and cyclohexane, and toluene and methylcyclohexane.
The acyclic hydrocarbons show a similar trend, although not as pronounced. The most prominent is seen in the i-hexane adsorption profiles, which show a more rapid increase of adsorption within CUB-30 at \(0.35\;\mathsf{kPa}\) and a preferential switch in adsorption within MUF-77 at \(1.2\;\mathsf{kPa}\) (Figure S20). In contrast, n-hexane adsorption shows a slight preference for adsorption within CUB-30 from \(0.30-0.36\;\mathsf{kPa}\), however this is short lived and does not exhibit substantial favourability for CUB-30.
## Conclusion
This research illustrates the major property changes and effects that can be brought about by a minor change in a multicomponent framework while preserving their attractive characteristics. Cubane-1,4-dicarboxylate (cdc) successfully forms analogues of well-known ternary and quaternary multicomponent MOFs, despite being shorter in length compared with benzene-1,4-dicarboxylate (bdc). Of significant interest is the resultant effect of varying the smallest ditopic linker in the MUF-77 framework, giving contoured pore environments in the two smallest pores of the framework. This results in a stark contrast in the hydrocarbon adsorption properties between the MUF-77 and CUB-30 frameworks. The higher number of methine units protruding into the small tetrahedral pore and the small dodecahedral pore result in an increased affinity for hydrocarbon molecules at low pressures to become adsorbed within the framework. Interestingly, the adsorption of cyclohexane at low pressures is greatly favored over the adsorption of benzene, unprecedented in MOF investigation and potentially addressing a common low pressure separation issue in the hydrogen transfer industry. The remarkable hydrocarbon separation properties that have emerged from this research verifies the important influence a contoured pore environment has on the behavior of the overall framework. This significant result is contrary to previous literature which explains the importance of numerous π–π interactions to underlie hydrocarbon adsorption in a MOF. This research, therefore, lays the foundation for future investigations into MOFs with contoured pore surfaces for selective hydrocarbon adsorption.
